\title{Non-Finite-Axiomatization of Measurable Majorities\\ via the Incoherence Index}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-27}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{coherence} of standard social decision frames --- finite sets
of admissible majority adjustments modeled as atoms in the cyclic group
$\Zn$ --- and ask whether coherence can be certified by any fixed, bounded family
of finite checks. We introduce the \emph{incoherence index} $\idx(F)$, the length
of the shortest \emph{perfectly balanced sequence} (a non-empty zero-sum word over
the atoms), and the \emph{width-$B$ fragment} $\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F)$, which a
frame passes when it admits no balanced sequence of length $\le B$. Our central
structural result is an exact threshold: for every incoherent frame,
\[
  \mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F) \iff B < \idx(F).
\]
From this we derive \emph{strict refinement}: for every $B$ there is a
\emph{maximal} frame passing the width-$B$ fragment but failing the width-$(B+1)$
fragment, so the hierarchy of finite fragments never collapses. Equivalently, the
coherence criterion is \emph{not finitely axiomatizable} by bounded fragments. The
engine is the single-generator frame $\{1\} \subseteq \Zn$, which is maximal and
whose incoherence index equals $n$ exactly --- the maximum attainable on $n$
states. We complement this with a saturation contrast --- the maximal frame
$\{1,3\} \subseteq \Z/4\Z$ has index only $2$ --- showing the extremal index is the
exclusive privilege of sparse generators, and with parity and unboundedness results
placing the index in additive combinatorics as a Davenport-constant-style
invariant. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

A persistent fantasy in the certification of decision procedures is the
\emph{finite checklist}: a fixed, bounded battery of local tests that, once passed,
guarantees a global consistency property. In software verification this is
``bounded model checking''; in logic it is ``finite axiomatizability''; in social
choice it is the hope that consistency of a voting rule can be witnessed by
inspecting only small coalitions or short preference cycles.

This paper isolates a clean arena where that fantasy is provably false, and false
in the strongest constructive sense. We model collective decisions over $n$ states
arranged cyclically and define coherence as the absence of any \emph{closed loop}
of admissible majority moves --- a chain that returns the collective to its
starting state. We then ask whether coherence is equivalent to the absence of
\emph{short} closed loops, for some uniform bound on length. The answer is no, and
we exhibit, for every candidate bound, an explicit legitimate (maximal) frame
defeating it.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A precise model of standard social decision frames as atom sets in $\Zn$,
        with the \emph{incoherence index} as the length of the shortest perfectly
        balanced sequence (Section~\ref{sec:model}).
  \item The exact-threshold theorem
        \texttt{coherentUpTo\_iff\_lt\_incoherenceIndex}: the width-$B$ fragment is
        passed iff $B < \idx(F)$ (Section~\ref{sec:threshold}).
  \item The strict-refinement theorem \texttt{fragment\_strictly\_refines} and its
        corollary, non-finite-axiomatizability of coherence
        (Section~\ref{sec:threshold}).
  \item The realization theorem \texttt{incoherenceIndex\_singleton\_one}: the
        single-generator frame $\{1\}$ is maximal with index exactly $n$, the
        greatest attainable (\texttt{incoherenceIndex\_isGreatest}), together with
        parity (\texttt{even\_incoherenceIndex}) and unboundedness
        (\texttt{incoherence\_unbounded}) (Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}).
  \item The saturation contrast \texttt{incoherenceIndex\_oneThree}: maximality
        alone does not determine the index (Section~\ref{sec:saturation}).
  \item The placement of the index within additive combinatorics as a
        Davenport-constant-style zero-sum invariant (Section~\ref{sec:zerosum}).
\end{enumerate}

All statements have been formally machine-checked.

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

Throughout, $n$ is a positive natural number and arithmetic is in the cyclic group
$\Zn$, whose elements are the residues $\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ with addition modulo
$n$.

\begin{definition}[Frame]
A \emph{standard social decision frame} on $n$ social states is a finite subset
$F \subseteq \Zn$. Its elements are called \emph{atoms} and represent admissible
majority-or-tie adjustments to the collective position. In the intended
interpretation the residue $0$ (``no change'') is not an atom, since it is never
itself a majority-driven move; this convention forces the index away from $1$ (see
Proposition~\ref{prop:notone}).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Perfectly balanced sequence]
A list $l = [x_1,\dots,x_k]$ is \emph{perfectly balanced} for $F$, written
$\bal(F,l)$, when (1) $l \neq []$; (2) every entry lies in $F$; and (3)
$x_1 + \cdots + x_k = 0$ in $\Zn$. Such a sequence is a \emph{closed loop}: a
non-trivial chain of admissible moves returning the collective to its origin.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Balanced lengths]
The set of attainable balanced-sequence lengths is
\[
  \blen(F) = \{\, k \in \N \mid \exists\, l,\; \bal(F,l) \wedge |l| = k \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Incoherence index]
The \emph{incoherence index} of $F$ is $\idx(F) = \inf \blen(F)$, the length of a
shortest perfectly balanced sequence, with the convention $\inf \emptyset = 0$.
Thus $\idx(F) = 0$ iff $F$ is \emph{coherent} (admits no balanced sequence), and
otherwise $\idx(F) \ge 1$ is the minimal loop length. A frame is \emph{incoherent}
when $\blen(F) \neq \emptyset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Maximality]
A frame $F$ is \emph{maximal}, written $\mathrm{IsMaximal}(F)$, when its atoms
generate the whole group: $\langle F \rangle = \Zn$. Maximal frames are the fully
expressive decision procedures: every state is reachable by some chain of
admissible moves.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Width-$B$ fragment]
For $B \in \N$, the frame $F$ is \emph{coherent up to $B$}, written
$\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F)$, when $\neg\,\exists\, l,\; \bal(F,l) \wedge |l| \le
B$; that is, $F$ admits no perfectly balanced sequence of length $\le B$.
\end{definition}

\section{The single-generator frame and the spectrum}\label{sec:spectrum}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{isMaximal\_singleton\_one}]\label{lem:max1}
For $n \ge 1$, the frame $\{1\} \subseteq \Zn$ is maximal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The subgroup generated by the unit $1$ contains every residue $x = x \cdot 1$, so
$\langle \{1\}\rangle = \top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{singleton\_one\_balanced\_dvd}]\label{lem:dvd}
If $l$ is perfectly balanced for $\{1\} \subseteq \Zn$, then $n \mid |l|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every entry lies in $\{1\}$, so $l = [1,\dots,1]$ with sum $|l|\cdot 1 = |l|
\pmod n$. Balancedness gives $|l| \equiv 0 \pmod n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{singleton\_one\_min\_length}]\label{lem:min}
If $l$ is perfectly balanced for $\{1\} \subseteq \Zn$, then $|l| \ge n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:dvd}, $n \mid |l|$, and $|l| > 0$ by non-emptiness; a positive
multiple of $n$ is at least $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[\texttt{incoherenceIndex\_le}]\label{prop:le}
For $n \ge 1$ and any non-empty frame $F$, $\idx(F) \le n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pick $a \in F$. The constant list $[a,\dots,a]$ of length $n$ has sum $n\cdot a = 0$,
hence is balanced, so $n \in \blen(F)$ and the infimum is $\le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{incoherenceIndex\_singleton\_one}]\label{thm:idx1}
For $n \ge 1$, $\idx(\{1\} \subseteq \Zn) = n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The bound $\le n$ is Proposition~\ref{prop:le}. For $\ge n$, every balanced length
is $\ge n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:min}, so the infimum is $\ge n$; the length-$n$
constant list realizes $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The index is never $1$]\label{prop:notone}
For a standard frame (excluding the atom $0$), $\idx(F) \neq 1$; hence
$\idx(F) \in \{0\} \cup \{2,3,\dots\}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A length-$1$ balanced sequence is a single atom $x = 0$, impossible since $0$ is
excluded; so $1 \notin \blen(F)$, and the index, being $0$ or $\ge 2$, is never
$1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness, \texttt{incoherenceIndex\_isGreatest}]\label{thm:great}
For every $n \ge 1$, $n$ is the greatest incoherence index attained by a non-empty
frame on $n$ states, and it is attained by $\{1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Attainment is Theorem~\ref{thm:idx1}; the upper bound is
Proposition~\ref{prop:le}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity, \texttt{even\_incoherenceIndex}]\label{thm:par}
Suppose $2 \mid n$ and every atom of $F$ maps to $1$ under the parity character
$\Zn \to \Z/2\Z$. Then $\idx(F)$ is even.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the parity homomorphism to a balanced sequence: the sum maps to $0$ in
$\Z/2\Z$, but equals $|l|\cdot 1 = |l| \pmod 2$, forcing $|l|$ even. So every
balanced length is even, and so is their minimum (or the index is $0$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness, \texttt{incoherence\_unbounded}]\label{thm:unb}
For every $N$ there is a frame whose incoherence index is even and exceeds $N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $\{1\} \subseteq \Z/(2N+4)\Z$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:idx1} its index is $2N+4 >
N$ and even.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:idx1}, \ref{thm:great}, \ref{thm:unb} with
Proposition~\ref{prop:notone}: the spectrum of incoherence indices is contained in
$\{0\} \cup \{2,3,\dots\}$, is unbounded above, and on $n$ states is capped exactly
at $n$, with the cap realized by the sparse generator $\{1\}$.

\section{The exact threshold and non-finite-axiomatization}\label{sec:threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Exact threshold, \texttt{coherentUpTo\_iff\_lt\_incoherenceIndex}]\label{thm:thresh}
Let $F$ be incoherent, i.e. $\blen(F) \neq \emptyset$. Then for every $B$,
\[
  \mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F) \iff B < \idx(F).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unfold $\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F)$ as ``no balanced sequence has length $\le B$.''

\noindent($\Rightarrow$) If $F$ passes the fragment yet $\idx(F) \le B$, then since
$\blen(F)$ is a non-empty set of naturals its infimum is attained: a balanced $l$
has $|l| = \idx(F) \le B$, contradicting the fragment. Hence $B < \idx(F)$.

\noindent($\Leftarrow$) Contrapositive: if $F$ fails the fragment, some balanced
$l$ has $|l| \le B$, so $\idx(F) \le |l| \le B$ (the infimum is a lower bound).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The incoherence hypothesis is essential: a coherent frame has index $0$ yet passes
\emph{every} fragment, so the equivalence genuinely requires an actual violation.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Strict refinement, \texttt{fragment\_strictly\_refines}]\label{thm:refine}
For every $B$ there exist $n$ and a frame $F$ on $n$ states with
$\mathrm{IsMaximal}(F)$, $\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,F)$, and
$\neg\,\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B+1,F)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $n = B+1$ and $F = \{1\} \subseteq \Z/(B+1)\Z$. Maximality is
Lemma~\ref{lem:max1}. By Theorem~\ref{thm:idx1} the index is $B+1$, so every
balanced sequence has length $\ge B+1 > B$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:min}) --- it passes
width $B$. The constant list $[1,\dots,1]$ of length $B+1$ is balanced and has
length $\le B+1$ --- it fails width $B+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-finite-axiomatizability of coherence]\label{cor:nfa}
There is no bound $B$ such that the width-$B$ fragment agrees with coherence on all
maximal frames. The family $\{\mathrm{CoherentUpTo}(B,\cdot)\}_{B \ge 0}$ is a
strictly increasing chain whose limit (true coherence) is not any finite member.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $B$. Theorem~\ref{thm:refine} yields a maximal frame $F$ that is incoherent (it
has a balanced sequence of length $B+1$) yet passes the width-$B$ fragment; so the
fragment certifies a non-coherent frame and cannot coincide with coherence. As $B$
is arbitrary and the separators $\{1\} \subseteq \Z/(B+1)\Z$ are distinct, no finite
width suffices and the hierarchy never stabilizes.
\end{proof}

\section{Saturation contrast}\label{sec:saturation}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{isMaximal\_oneThree}]
The frame $\{1,3\} \subseteq \Z/4\Z$ is maximal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
It contains the unit $1$, which generates $\Z/4\Z$; adding atoms cannot shrink the
generated subgroup.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{incoherenceIndex\_oneThree}]
$\idx(\{1,3\} \subseteq \Z/4\Z) = 2$, strictly below the index $4$ of
$\{1\} \subseteq \Z/4\Z$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound: $[1,3]$ is balanced since $1+3 = 4 \equiv 0$. Lower bound: a length-$1$
balanced sequence needs an atom equal to $0$, but neither $1$ nor $3$ is $0$ in
$\Z/4\Z$; so every balanced sequence has length $\ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Both $\{1\}$ and $\{1,3\}$ are maximal on $\Z/4\Z$, so reachability is identical;
the gap $4$ versus $2$ is caused purely by atom density. Hence maximality alone does
not determine the index, and the extremal value $n$ is the exclusive privilege of
single-generator (sparse) frames.
\end{remark}

\section{The index as a zero-sum invariant}\label{sec:zerosum}

Stripped of its social-choice interpretation, a perfectly balanced sequence is a
non-empty \emph{zero-sum sequence} over $\Zn$ with terms restricted to $F$, and the
incoherence index is the minimal length of a zero-sum word over $F$ --- a
Davenport-constant-style invariant. This explains the phenomena structurally: the
cyclic computation $\idx(\{1\}) = n$ is the minimal-zero-sum length over a single
generator of order $n$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:dvd}); the saturation collapse reflects that
extra group elements create short zero-sums; and unboundedness reflects growth of
the invariant with the group order. The social-choice wrapper is thus incidental:
the non-finite-axiomatizability is governed by additive combinatorics of $\Zn$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\textbf{Social choice.} Coherence of a strict-majority decision procedure cannot be
certified by inspecting only short cycles of admissible moves; no fixed cycle-length
budget suffices.

\textbf{Bounded verification.} The result is a sharp parable for bounded model
checking: testing all behaviors up to depth $B$ cannot replace a full consistency
proof when violations can occur at unbounded depth.

\textbf{Logic.} Corollary~\ref{cor:nfa} is a constructive instance of
non-finite-axiomatizability via a strictly refining chain of finite fragments.

\section{Future work}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Spectrum over all frames.} Conjecture: the attainable values of
        $\idx(F)$ are exactly $\{0\}\cup\{2,3,\dots\}$; index $1$ is impossible
        (Proposition~\ref{prop:notone}).
  \item \emph{Davenport identification.} Conjecture: for a maximal frame in general
        position, $\idx(F) = D(\langle F\rangle)$, the Davenport constant of the
        generated subgroup; Lemma~\ref{lem:dvd} is the cyclic base case.
  \item \emph{Density collapses the index.} Conjecture: a maximal frame with
        $\ge c\log n$ well-spread atoms has $\idx(F) = 2$.
  \item \emph{No eventual completeness.} Conjecture: no $B$ and no finite exception
        set make the width-$B$ fragment agree with coherence off the exceptions;
        the separators of Theorem~\ref{thm:refine} are a fresh non-isomorphic
        family.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The incoherence index is the complete invariant controlling the finite fragments of
the coherence criterion. The exact-threshold theorem shows a frame survives the
width-$B$ test precisely when its shortest violation exceeds $B$; the
strict-refinement theorem then shows the fragments refine strictly and forever,
driven by the elementary computation $\idx(\{1\}) = n$. Coherence therefore admits
no bounded finite axiomatization. The saturation contrast localizes the extremal
behavior to sparse generators, and the additive-combinatorial reading recasts the
story as one about minimal zero-sum lengths.

